Practical Use Cases

1. Failed Login Detection
Generate failed SSH logins on Ubuntu:

ssh wronguser@ubuntu-server


Repeat several times.
Wazuh alert:

Rule ID: 5710

Multiple authentication failures detected

Level: High



SOC Analyst Action:
◇ Check the source IP.
◇ Determine if it is an internal mistake or an external brute-force attempt.
◇ Block the IP if malicious.

2. File Integrity Monitoring (FIM)
Modify a monitored file:



sudo nano /etc/passwd

Save the file.
Wazuh generates an alert:

File Modified

/etc/passwd

Severity: High



SOC Analyst Action:
◇ Verify who made the change.
◇ Compare the new and previous versions.
◇ Confirm whether it was authorized.


3. Malware Detection
Copy a known test malware file such as the EICAR test file to the monitored endpoint.
Alert:

Malware Detected

EICAR Test File

Severity: Critical


SOC Analyst Action:
◇ Isolate the endpoint if needed.
◇ Remove the file.
◇ Investigate how it was introduced.


4. Root Login Detection
Edit the SSH configuration to permit root login (for testing only), then log in as root.
Alert:

Successful Root Login

Severity: Medium


SOC Analyst Action:
◇ Verify whether root access is expected.
◇ Check the source IP and user activity.


5. USB Detection (Windows)
Insert a USB drive.
Alert:

USB Device Connected

Device Name

Kingston DataTraveler


SOC Analyst Action:
◇ Confirm whether USB usage is allowed.
◇ Check for data transfers if necessary.


6. New User Created
Linux:



sudo adduser hacker


Alert:

New User Created

Username

hacker



SOC Analyst Action:
◇ Verify who created the account.
◇ Remove unauthorized accounts.
◇ Review related administrative actions.


Typical SOC Investigation Workflow
1. Alert Received – Wazuh raises an alert.
2. Validate – Check if it is a true positive or false positive.
3. Investigate – Review logs, affected host, user, and timeline.
4. Contain – Isolate the endpoint or block the attacker if necessary.
5. Eradicate – Remove malware, disable malicious accounts, or fix the issue.
6. Recover – Restore normal operations and verify systems.
7. Document – Record findings and lessons learned.

